# Caching, and why the two rules below must disagree.
#
# This is the root of the stale-chunk failure, not just a performance setting. index.html is the one
# file that names the current build's hashed entry bundle. If a browser keeps a cached copy, it goes
# on booting last week's entry bundle, which asks for chunk hashes the newest deploy has already
# replaced — and every lazy route 404s. Revalidating index.html on each visit is what makes a deploy
# actually reach the user.
#
# Everything under /assets is content-hashed by Vite: a changed file gets a new name, so the old one
# can be cached forever and never needs revalidating. That is what `immutable` is for.

/index.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
